

CS 252 Lab 3 – Unix Shell
Student: Naman Kansal (kansal2)

FEATURES IMPLEMENTED

Part 1: Core Shell Functionality
• Custom Unix-style command shell implemented in C++
• Execution of external programs using fork() and execvp()
• Support for commands with multiple arguments
• Background execution using &
• Proper parent–child process synchronization using wait()/waitpid()

---

Part 2: Command Parsing
• Lexical analysis of user input using Flex (Lex)
• Grammar-based parsing using Bison (Yacc)
• Parsing of commands, arguments, pipes, and redirections
• Structured internal representation of commands using command objects
• Robust handling of malformed or incomplete commands

---

Part 3: Pipes
• Support for single and multiple pipelines
• Correct setup and teardown of pipe file descriptors
• Chaining of commands using |
• Proper duplication of file descriptors using dup2()
• Isolation of pipeline stages into separate processes

---

Part 4: Input and Output Redirection
• Input redirection using <
• Output redirection using >
• Output append using >>
• Error redirection using 2>
• Validation of redirection targets and file permissions

---

Part 5: Built-in Commands
• cd (change directory)
• exit (terminate shell execution)
• Built-ins executed without spawning child processes

---

Part 6: Process Management and Error Handling
• Correct handling of background and foreground processes
• Cleanup of zombie processes
• Graceful handling of failed system calls
• Descriptive error messages for invalid commands

---

CONCEPTS DEMONSTRATED
• UNIX process creation and management
• Inter-process communication using pipes
• File descriptor manipulation
• Lexical analysis and parsing
• Shell command execution model
• Error handling in system-level programs

---

BUILDING AND RUNNING

Build the shell
make

Run the shell
./shell

Execute commands
command [arguments]
command1 | command2 | command3
command > output.txt
command < input.txt
command &

